----+----+----+----+\n     a    b    c    d    e    f    g    h \nYour valid moves are (src-dst-promotion, or src-dst):\n[P: a2-a4, P: a2-a3, N: b1-a3, N: b1-c3, P: b2-b4, P: b2-b3, P: c2-c4, P: c2-c3, Q: d1-e2, Q: d1-f3, Q: d1-g4, Q: d1-h5, P: d2-d4, P: d2-d3, K: e1-e2, P: e4-e5, B: f1-e2, B: f1-d3, B: f1-c4, B: f1-b5, B: f1-a6, P: f2-f4, P: f2-f3, P: g2-g4, P: g2-g3, N: g5-e6, N: g5-f3, N: g5-f7, N: g5-h3, N: g5-h7, R: h1-g1, P: h2-h4, P: h2-h3]\nYour answer format should be: Answer: piece-name: move. \nPiece-name and move (src-dst-promotion or src-dst) should be directly copied EXACTLY from valid move list.\nExample of valid output format: N: a1-b3; P: e7-e8-q; Piece B: g5-f4.\nPlease select a move from your valid moves that maximize your probability of winning.\n', 'reasoning': '', 'output': "Evaluation: Let me analyze the current position. Black has developed both knights (c6, f6) and pushed the h-pawn to h6, while I have advanced my e-pawn to e4 and my knight to g5. \n\nKey considerations:\n1. **N: g5-f7** - This is a very strong fork move! The knight on f7 would attack both the Black queen on d8 and the rook on h8. This is a classic knight fork pattern.\n\n2. **N: g5-e6** - Attacks the queen and controls key central squares, but the knight could be captured and doesn't win material immediately.\n\n3. **Q: d1-h5** - Attacks h6 and f7, putting pressure on Black's kingside, but Black can defend with moves like Ng6.\n\n4. **B: f1-c4** or **B: f1-b5** - Good developing moves, but don't create immediate tactical threats.\n\n5. **P: d2-d4** - Strong central pawn push for development, but misses the tactical opportunity.\n\nThe knight fork on f7 is clearly the strongest move. After Nf7, Black cannot save both the queen and rook. Even if Black moves the queen, I win the exchange (rook for knight), gaining significant material advantage.\n\nReason: The knight fork Ng5-f7 wins material by force. The knight simultaneously attacks the Black queen on d8 and the rook on h8. Black must move the queen to safety, allowing me to capture the rook on h8 with Nxh8, winning the exchange and gaining a decisive material advantage.\n\nAnswer: N: g5-f7"}
